{"@context":["https://litlfred.github.io/folio-assistant/ns/content/v1.jsonld",{"@base":"https://litlfred.github.io/folio/"}],"@id":"papers/quantum-observable-universe/blocks/rem-q-angular-momentum-interpretation","@type":["folio-assistant-core:Remark","doco:Section"],"label":"rem:q-angular-momentum-interpretation","kind":"remark","title":"Jones polynomial connection, building block values, and nuclear $q$-angular momentum","uses":["papers/quantum-observable-universe/blocks/def-q-angular-momentum"],"interprets":"papers/quantum-observable-universe/blocks/def-q-angular-momentum","tags":["angular-momentum","jones-polynomial","nuclear","spin","q-deformed"],"text":"q-angular-momentum-interpretation.md","provenance":"authored","html":"<p><a id=\"rem:q-angular-momentum-interpretation\"></a>\n<strong>Remark.</strong> <em>Jones polynomial connection, building block values, and nuclear <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">q</code>-angular momentum</em></p>\n<p><strong>Connection to the Jones polynomial.</strong></p>\n<p>The Jones polynomial <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">J_K(q)</code> of a knot <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">K</code> is the quantum trace over the spin-<code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\tfrac{1}{2}</code> representation:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-math math-display\">  J_K(q) =  \\mathrm{tr}_{V_{1/2}}(\\rho(\\beta))</code></pre>\n<p>The colored Jones polynomial <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">J_K^{(j)}(q)</code> uses the spin-<code class=\"language-math math-inline\">j</code> representation. The <strong>recursion</strong> between colored Jones polynomials encodes the <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">q</code>-angular momentum:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-math math-display\">  J_K^{(j+1/2)}(q) =  [2j+1]_q \\cdot J_K^{(j)}(q)\n  +  \\text{(lower-order terms)}</code></pre>\n<p><strong>Building block values.</strong></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Knot</th>\n<th align=\"center\"><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">n</code> (strands)</th>\n<th align=\"center\"><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">j</code></th>\n<th align=\"center\"><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">J_q^2</code> at the substrate parameter <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">q</code></th>\n<th align=\"center\">Classical <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">j(j+1)</code></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">0_1</code> (unknot)</td>\n<td align=\"center\"><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">1</code></td>\n<td align=\"center\"><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">0</code></td>\n<td align=\"center\"><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">0</code></td>\n<td align=\"center\"><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">0</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">3_1</code> (trefoil)</td>\n<td align=\"center\"><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">2</code></td>\n<td align=\"center\"><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\frac{1}{2}</code></td>\n<td align=\"center\"><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">[1/2]_q [3/2]_q = 0.7507</code></td>\n<td align=\"center\"><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">0.75</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">4_1</code> (figure-eight)</td>\n<td align=\"center\"><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">2</code></td>\n<td align=\"center\"><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\frac{1}{2}</code></td>\n<td align=\"center\"><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">0.7507</code></td>\n<td align=\"center\"><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">0.75</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">5_1</code> (cinquefoil)</td>\n<td align=\"center\"><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">2</code></td>\n<td align=\"center\"><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\frac{1}{2}</code></td>\n<td align=\"center\"><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">0.7507</code></td>\n<td align=\"center\"><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">0.75</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\mathcal{B}_3</code> (Borromean)</td>\n<td align=\"center\"><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">3</code></td>\n<td align=\"center\"><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\frac{1}{2}</code></td>\n<td align=\"center\"><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">0.7507</code></td>\n<td align=\"center\"><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">0.75</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>All single-component knots in <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">B_2</code> carry spin <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">j = \\tfrac{1}{2}</code>. The nuclear spin depends on coupling: <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">A</code> nucleons (each <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">j = 1/2</code>) couple to total <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">J</code> via <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">q</code>-Clebsch--Gordan coefficients. The parity of <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">A</code> (modulo 2) determines the <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">J</code> parity (<a href=\"#prop:nuclear-spin-parity\">nuclear spin parity</a>).</p>\n<p><strong>Nuclear <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">q</code>-angular momentum.</strong></p>\n<p>For a nucleus <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\mathcal{N}(Z, N)</code> with <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">A = Z + N</code>:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-math math-display\">  J_q^2(\\mathcal{N}) =  [J]_q  [J+1]_q</code></pre>\n<p>Here <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">J</code> is the ground-state spin fixed by the nuclear link's embedding in the Temperley--Lieb algebra <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">TL_A(\\delta)</code> with <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\delta = -(q^{1/2} + q^{-1/2})</code>.</p>\n"}
